<div class="page">

  <h1>Likutay Halachos</h1>
  <h1>Choshen Mishpat II &mdash; Laws of Gifts (<em class="hterm">Matanah</em>) 3</h1>

  <h2 class="halacha-title"><em>The matter of a deathbed-bequest gift [<em class="hterm">matnas shchiv mera</em>] and what our Sages, of blessed memory, said:</em></h2>
  <h2 class="halacha-subtitle"><em>The Sages equated a deathbed-bequest gift with an inheritance [<em class="hterm">yerushah</em>], and the words of a deathbed-bequeather are considered as [if they were already] written and delivered.</em></h2>

  <hr class="rule-line"/>

  <!-- ============================================================ SECTION 1 -->
  <div class="section-header">1</div>

  <p>
    Based upon the Torah that begins <span class="verse">"And it came to pass at the end [<em class="hterm">mikaitz</em>]
    — for He who has compassion on them shall lead them"</span>, etc.
    <span class="source">[<em>Likutay Moharan</em> II, 7; cf. Isaiah 49:10]</span> — see there.
    And we have already spoken about this Torah several times.
  </p>

  <p>
    And it is explained there that every person must leave behind his <em class="hterm">da'as</em>
    [knowledge / mind / awareness] in the world through sons and disciples,
    engaging with them to make known to them the way of G-d —
    and this is the essential exchange and legacy that remains from a person after his departure from the world, etc.;
    see there. And it is explained there that through the illumination of sons and disciples,
    from there sustenance [<em class="hterm">parnasah</em>] is drawn, etc.; see there.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ SECTION 2 -->
  <hr class="rule-line"/>
  <div class="section-header">2</div>

  <p>
    And this is the aspect of <em class="hterm">yerushah</em> [inheritance] that belongs to sons —
    for the essential legacy a person leaves after his death is his sons,
    who remain after him and fill his place.
    And the essence is the <em class="hterm">da'as</em> [knowledge/awareness] —
    meaning that when one merits it,
    there remain after him sons who received from him his holy <em class="hterm">da'as</em>.
    For every one of Israel has a share in the holy <em class="hterm">da'as</em>,
    for the essential <em class="hterm">da'as</em> is to know G-d,
    as it is written: <span class="verse">"You have been shown to know that Hashem is G-d, etc."</span>
    <span class="source">[Deuteronomy 4:35]</span>
    And every one of Israel is a portion of G-d from above
    <span class="source">[cf. Job 31:2; <em>Tikkunay Zohar</em>]</span>,
    and his soul is rooted in this holy <em class="hterm">da'as</em>,
    which is the root of all souls — for <em class="hterm">Chachmah</em> [Wisdom] is the root of all,
    as it is written: <span class="verse">"You made them all with wisdom [<em class="hterm">Chachmah</em>]"</span>
    <span class="source">[Psalms 104:24]</span>.
  </p>

  <p>
    And therefore, every one of Israel — according to his attachment to the holy <em class="hterm">da'as</em>
    and according to his good deeds — so is the knowledge of G-d's divinity, blessed be He,
    revealed through him. And this is the essential legacy after his death —
    namely, what he made known to his sons and commanded them, that they keep the way of G-d.
    And this <em class="hterm">da'as</em> is the essential legacy,
    as is explained in the aforementioned Torah.
  </p>

  <p>
    And since all sustenance and wealth is drawn from this <em class="hterm">da'as</em>
    — which is the aspect encompassing [both] son and disciple,
    as is explained there in the aforementioned Torah; see there —
    therefore, when a person passes away, the inheritance reaches his sons,
    for the root of wealth derives from the aspect of sons — meaning from the <em class="hterm">da'as</em>
    he left in the world through his sons,
    from which all wealth and sustenance is drawn, as stated above.
    And therefore the inheritance reaches them,
    for from there the wealth is drawn, as stated above.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ SECTION 3 -->
  <hr class="rule-line"/>
  <div class="section-header">3</div>

  <p>
    And this is [the nature of] a <em class="hterm">matnas shchiv mera</em>
    [a deathbed-bequest gift] which is also like an inheritance.
    For the <em class="hterm">da'as</em> that one must leave behind
    is comprised of two aspects — [that of] son and [that of] disciple —
    for one must leave behind sons and disciples, as is explained there in the aforementioned Torah.
    For just as one must teach one's own sons — to illuminate within them the holy <em class="hterm">da'as</em>
    — so too must one teach another's son and establish many disciples,
    to illuminate within them the holy <em class="hterm">da'as</em> to know G-d.
    And as is explained there in the aforementioned Torah,
    every person can fulfill this — to establish disciples —
    by speaking with his fellow about fear of Heaven,
    and one arousing the other to fear G-d, to turn from evil and do good,
    so that through this each one becomes in the aspect of a disciple to the other;
    see there.
  </p>

  <p>
    And from the aspect of the <em class="hterm">da'as</em> that one illuminates within disciples —
    each person according to his aspect —
    from there is drawn the aspect of <em class="hterm">matnas shchiv mera</em> that is like an inheritance.
    For a person has the power to bequeath his wealth to another through a
    <em class="hterm">matnas shchiv mera</em>,
    since wealth is drawn also from the <em class="hterm">da'as</em> that one illuminates within others
    who are not his sons — who are the disciples.
    And therefore he can bequeath his wealth to others through a <em class="hterm">matnas shchiv mera</em>,
    just as he must illuminate his <em class="hterm">da'as</em> to others who are the disciples.
    For just as the inheritance belongs to the sons — because wealth is drawn from the
    <em class="hterm">da'as</em> he left through his sons —
    so too can he bequeath his wealth to others,
    for wealth is drawn also from the <em class="hterm">da'as</em> that one illuminates within others,
    who are the disciples, as stated above.
    For wealth is drawn from both aspects — from son and from disciple —
    as is explained in the aforementioned Torah.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ SECTION 4 -->
  <hr class="rule-line"/>
  <div class="section-header">4</div>

  <p>
    And therefore, specifically when he is a <em class="hterm">shchiv mera</em>
    [one who is deathly ill / bedridden], his gift is like an inheritance.
    For the <em class="hterm">tzadik</em>, and likewise every upright person,
    illuminates the <em class="hterm">da'as</em> within sons and disciples all the days of his life —
    for it is only for this that a person comes into the world.
    And the essential [goal] is to draw such a <em class="hterm">da'as</em>
    into sons and disciples that it will endure forever,
    even after his passing — for this is the essential legacy, as stated above,
    and as is explained clearly in the aforementioned Torah.
  </p>

  <p>
    And therefore, the true <em class="hterm">tzadikim</em>,
    at the time of their illness, illuminate the <em class="hterm">da'as</em>
    even more intensely within sons and disciples —
    as we find with several <em class="hterm">tzadikim</em> who revealed a very great and holy
    <em class="hterm">da'as</em> at the time of their illness, near their death —
    such as Rabbi Eliezer the Great, Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai,
    and Rabbenu HaKadosh [our Holy Teacher, Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi], etc.
    <span class="source">[cf. Sanhedrin 68a; Shabbos 33b; Kesubos 103a]</span>
  </p>

  <p>
    For the essential illness that is drawn into the world — that a person is ill before his death —
    is only for this reason: so that he may set his household in order,
    <span class="verse">"so that he may command his sons and his disciples after him"</span>
    <span class="source">[cf. Genesis 18:19]</span>
    that they know G-d and keep His ways.
    And therefore, until Yaakov there was no weakness [before death],
    as our Sages, of blessed memory, stated
    <span class="source">[Bava Metzia 87a]</span>
    — for Yaakov was the first to illuminate so intensely within sons and disciples.
    For the beginning of the revelation of the holy <em class="hterm">da'as</em>
    — to know G-d — began with our father Avraham,
    for he revealed G-d's divinity in the world, as our Sages, of blessed memory, stated.
    But Avraham and Yitzchak still did not have their bed [lineage] complete —
    only Yaakov's bed was complete,
    and he left behind twelve tribes, all of them <em class="hterm">tzadikim</em>,
    all of whom received his holy <em class="hterm">da'as</em>,
    and through them the holy <em class="hterm">da'as</em> was sustained in the world.
  </p>

  <p>
    And therefore it began with Yaakov that a person should be ill before his death —
    for it is a great good, so that he may set his household in order
    and command his sons, as our Sages, of blessed memory, stated.
    And the essential commandment is that one must command them to keep the way of G-d,
    so that his <em class="hterm">da'as</em> will remain even after his passing, forever.
    And therefore this began with Yaakov,
    for he was the first to leave behind sons who were all <em class="hterm">tzadikim</em>,
    through whom his <em class="hterm">da'as</em> remained in the world forever.
  </p>

  <p>
    And therefore, at the time when Yaakov was ill and commanded his sons near his death,
    they all cried out: <span class="verse">"Hear O Israel [<em class="hterm">Shema Yisrael</em>],
    Hashem is our G-d, Hashem is One"</span>, etc.,
    as our Sages, of blessed memory, stated
    <span class="source">[Pesachim 56a]</span>
    — for at that time, when the <em class="hterm">tzadik</em> is ill near his death,
    that is the essential illumination of the <em class="hterm">da'as</em>
    that he leaves behind after him, as stated above.
    And likewise for every person, according to his own aspect —
    for every person can fulfill this.
    And every person must illuminate some aspect of holy <em class="hterm">da'as</em>
    within sons and disciples, so that it endures forever, as stated above.
  </p>

  <p>
    And therefore, the essential gift that is like an inheritance is specifically
    the <em class="hterm">matnas shchiv mera</em> —
    for it is then that the essential leaving-behind of the <em class="hterm">da'as</em> occurs,
    from which wealth is drawn.
    And because of this the inheritance reaches his sons,
    and likewise he has the power to give to others through a <em class="hterm">matnas shchiv mera</em>
    which is also like an inheritance, as stated above.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ SECTION 5 -->
  <hr class="rule-line"/>
  <div class="section-header">5</div>

  <p>
    And this is [what is meant by]: <em>"the words of a <em class="hterm">shchiv mera</em>
    are as [if they were already] written and delivered"</em>
    <span class="source">[<em>Bava Basra</em> 135b; <em>Gittin</em> 13a]</span>
    — for the validity of the <em class="hterm">matnas shchiv mera</em> is drawn
    from the aspect of the <em class="hterm">da'as</em>
    that one must illuminate with even greater intensity near one's death,
    when one is a <em class="hterm">shchiv mera</em>, as stated above.
    And the <em class="hterm">da'as</em> is the Torah,
    which is the essential <em class="hterm">da'as</em> through which one knows G-d, blessed be He.
  </p>

  <p>
    And this is: <em>"the words of a <em class="hterm">shchiv mera</em>
    are as written and delivered"</em> —
    <em>"written and delivered"</em> is the aspect of the holy Torah,
    which is written [<em class="hterm">kesuvah</em>] and delivered [<em class="hterm">mesurah</em>]
    in our hands.
    And every person, according to his portion in the Torah,
    so must he leave behind his portion of <em class="hterm">da'as</em> and Torah
    to sons and disciples so that it endures forever.
    And from there is drawn the <em class="hterm">matnas shchiv mera</em>
    that has validity like an inheritance, as stated above.
    And therefore <em>"the words of a <em class="hterm">shchiv mera</em>
    are as written and delivered,"</em> as stated above.
  </p>

