  <!-- ============================================================ SUMMARY -->
  <hr class="rule-line"/>
  <div class="summary-box">
    <h3>&#9733; Translator's Summary &mdash; Matanah 3 &#9733;</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The core theme — <em class="hterm">da'as</em> as legacy:</strong>
        Drawing from <em>Likutay Moharan</em> II, 7, R. Nosson teaches that a person's true
        legacy is not wealth but <em class="hterm">da'as</em> — the knowledge and awareness of G-d —
        passed on to sons and disciples. From this <em class="hterm">da'as</em>, all wealth and
        sustenance is ultimately drawn.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Why inheritance goes to sons:</strong>
        Wealth flows from the <em class="hterm">da'as</em> a father leaves embedded in his sons.
        Sons who carry their father's holy <em class="hterm">da'as</em> are the natural heirs,
        since they are literally the conduit through which that wealth-generating spiritual force
        continues in the world.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Why a deathbed gift also carries the force of inheritance:</strong>
        Wealth flows not only through sons, but also through <em>disciples</em> — anyone to
        whom a person transmitted <em class="hterm">da'as</em>. A <em class="hterm">matnas shchiv mera</em>
        therefore has the legal weight of an inheritance, because it parallels the teacher–disciple
        transmission of <em class="hterm">da'as</em>, just as inheritance parallels the father–son
        transmission.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Illness before death is a gift, not a curse:</strong>
        The tradition that illness began with Yaakov (the first whose entire lineage was wholly
        righteous) reveals its deeper purpose — to grant a person time to command sons and disciples.
        The scene of the twelve tribes calling out <em class="hterm">Shema Yisrael</em> at Yaakov's
        deathbed is the archetype of this peak illumination of <em class="hterm">da'as</em>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>"Written and delivered" = Torah:</strong>
        The legal formula that a dying person's words have the force of a written and delivered
        document is grounded in Torah itself — which is the ultimate "written and delivered"
        transmission. Just as Torah endures forever, so does the deathbed gift carry eternal
        validity.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cross-references:</strong> Halachah 4 is continued in Matanah 4, section 15;
        Halachah 5 is continued in Matanah 5, section 23.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

